Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.510 align:start position:0%
 
for years there's been this science  fiction trope of conflict in the future  being defined by large numbers of  swarming drones or autonomous systems  but it's not just a sci-fi trope this is  also what a lot of war planners and a  lot of people looking at the future of  the military believe  is going to be the future anaerobial  industries is a defense product company  that builds defense technology and then  sells it to the united states government  and our allies around the world the  thing that makes us special and the  thing that makes us different from a lot  of other contractors is that we use our  own money to fund almost all of our  research and development and we build  products and then we sell them after  they're done so we're usually not going  to the government saying here's an idea  here's a white paper you're going to pay  for it whether it works or not we're  saying look we are going to use our own  money on this we're going to make sure  that it works and no matter what if  you're spending money on it it is a sure  thing  our military industrial base the large  defense primes that are willing to work  with the dod don't have the best people  tools or talent in areas like autonomy  robotics sensor fusion high-end  networking immersive displays and what i  wanted to do was build a new company  where we could take people out of roles  in big tech and put them to work helping  with national security  so my last company was oculus vr i  started it when i was 19 years old  living in a camper trailer something  that not a lot of people know is that  before starting oculus which everyone  does know about i actually worked in an  army affiliate research center the ict  mixed reality lab at the university of  southern california and one of the  things that i worked on was a project  called brave mind and i was not by any  means really deeply involved with the  day-to-day that program i was just a lab  technician a tech monkey making a lot of  the head-mounted displays work but that  was a program that was using virtual  reality exposure therapy to treat  veterans with ptsd in the us army and  that was the first time that i was  really exposed to how the dod works with  technology how they think about  technology and it always stuck in my  head that was something i'd like to  contribute to that i would like to make  better you know there was there were a  lot of things that were broken despite a  lot of the good that was being done  the goal of and role is to become the  next major defense prime and one of the  ways that we thought about this is  figuring out what would a major defense  problem look like they started today if  you were reading science fiction set in  the future what would the back story of  a company like andrew b what kinds of  technologies would it have built what  would make it believable and it's  clearly exactly the things that we're  working on autonomy artificial  intelligence swarming systems really  high end command and control software  one thing that people don't realize is  the android is already directly saving  lives we're already  keeping people alive using our  technology who would not be alive today  if it wasn't for our technology for one  example we make security systems that  are used to protect military bases  around the world from hostile incursions  we make technology that's used to  protect people from getting attacked by  drones and these aren't hypotheticals  these aren't things that we want to do  someday we're sending systems into  combat that are protecting people from  hostile incursions and hostile drone  attacks and i can't talk about the  details for obvious reasons but people  should realize that this isn't a  hypothetical that our tech might someday  do some good it is doing incredibly good  things right now today as we speak  andrew does business differently because  we only get paid when we make things  that work not when we do work most  contracts the duty gives out are what's  called a cost plus contract you get paid  for time materials hours and then a  fixed percentage of profit on top which  means you're naturally incentivized as a  major defense company to  make things that are as expensive as  possible to work as long as possible i'm  not saying that people wake up in the  morning and say ah another day another  chance to screw the u.s taxpayer but you  naturally end up trending in that  direction because you make more money  when your project goes longer more money  when you come up with more expensive  things in android we don't look at it  that way because we're funding the early  development of our own programs we  wanted to find the most effective thing  at the most cost-effective price point  and then work as quickly as possible so  that we can actually sell that and that  difference in incentives is what drives  the difference in results  working the defense space requires a lot  of different and varied space it's not  like a software startup where you can  just have a few guys and a few laptops  and you just bang out on the keyboard in  your living room a lot of great  companies have been built that way but  to work in the defense space you have to  have cleared space where you can work on  sensitive projects to government  standards you have to be able to build  hardware to be able to build software  you have to be able to host  demonstrations of your capabilities you  need to be able to burn things in and  test them in a really robust fashion you  can't afford for things to fail you  can't really move fast and break things  when lives are on the line one of the  biggest challenges we had breaking into  this industry is that it is an industry  that is really dominated by a handful of  large primes who then subcontract all of  their work to much smaller vendors we  wanted to be the next major prime the  goal of anderl from day one was to save  taxpayers hundreds of billions of  dollars a year by making tens of  billions of dollars a year making  cheaper technology that does better  things and being the prime on it getting  to decide how it's built how it's  contracted how it's sold as a new  entrant it's really really hard to get  the government to take you seriously you  really have to build things that are so  good that they can't ignore you you  can't just be better than what they're  using you have to be so much radically  better that they have to pay attention  to it if i was learning how to run a  company for the first time alongside all  that there's no way that andrew would  have been successful it was hugely  helpful that i've actually started a  multi-billion dollar company before the  defense space is definitely not a space  where i would encourage first-time  founders to look to i understand why  they do it it's because they're  patriotic because they want to make a  difference they want to work on  something more important than augmented  reality mustache emojis but this is a  really really tough business and i would  strongly encourage people to get some  business to get some experience out in  the real world before you try to live in  the bizarro world that is defense  contracting  the united states is in a really  interesting position right now where the  technology companies that really  dominate our tech industry largely  refuse to do work with the us department  of defense there's never been a point in  history where that was the case and that  would be okay if it was just in a vacuum  i don't mind us actually having a weak  military if we don't need a military the  problem is that we live in a time where  major powers like russia and china are  heavily investing in exactly the types  of technologies that we are not good at  building people laugh at china because  they don't have a large blue water navy  or maybe because we have a bigger air  force or a more powerful air force we  need to realize they're not planning on  winning by building the tools that we  use to win the last few wars they're  going to build asymmetrical capabilities  largely built on top of autonomous  systems and then they're going to use  their manufacturing advantages to come  after us especially  especially china and so that's the  reason that i really became  really fearful because we live in this  situation where the united states has  always had an edge we've always had the  strongest military we've always had the  best technology our partners have always  relied on us largely because of that and  we're now living in an area where some  of our strategic adversaries are rushing  ahead of us with technology like  artificial intelligence  the fundamental goal of every company  working the defense base should be to  deter conflict most conflicts occur when  one or both sides  miscalculate the odds of winning when  both sides agree that a conflict either  can be won or cannot be one you they  tend to be resolved through more  diplomatic means and so the goal of the  united states has largely been not to  ensure that we will win wars against  every person that we might ever fight  with it's to prevent them from ever even  wanting to fight us so that conflict is  unthinkable if you don't start working  on technology for the dod until after  the conflict starts you ensure that  whatever you build will only be a part  of the conflict a part of the bloodshed  it is not going to be a part of  deterring the conflict from happening in  the first place that's what anderl's  trying to do we're trying to build the  tools that make warfare unthinkable

